{"name":"Raccoon Tail","hex":"#735e56","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"raccoon tail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hair extension with horizontal stripes, associated with the emo scene.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see raccoon,‎ tail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raccoon_tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Raccoon","blocks":[{"text":"The raccoon, sometimes called the North American, northern or common raccoon to distinguish it from other species of raccoon, is a mammal native to North America. It is the largest of the procyonid family, having a body length of 40 to 70 cm, and a body weight of 5 to 26 kg. Its grayish coat mostly consists of dense underfur, which insulates it against cold weather. The animal's most distinctive features include its extremely dexterous front paws, its facial mask, and its ringed tail, which are common themes in the mythologies of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas surrounding the species. The raccoon is noted for its intelligence, and studies show that it can remember the solution to tasks for at least three years. It is usually nocturnal and omnivorous, eating about 40% invertebrates, 33% plants, and 27% vertebrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg/330px-Raccoon_in_Central_Park_%2835264%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raccoon"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Raccoon-range.png/500px-Raccoon-range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Raccoon-range.png/1280px-Raccoon-range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Raccoon-range.png/500px-Raccoon-range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARaccoon-range.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Raccoon_%28Procyon_lotor%29_2.jpg/500px-Raccoon_%28Procyon_lotor%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Raccoon_%28Procyon_lotor%29_2.jpg/500px-Raccoon_%28Procyon_lotor%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The mask of a raccoon is often interrupted by a brown-black streak that extends from forehead to nose.[6]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARaccoon_(Procyon_lotor)_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Raccoons_of_North_%26_Middle_America_%281950%29_Skins_of_subgenera_procyon_%26_euprocyon.png/330px-Raccoons_of_North_%26_Middle_America_%281950%29_Skins_of_subgenera_procyon_%26_euprocyon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Raccoons_of_North_%26_Middle_America_%281950%29_S